  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001263",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches via trait_efo including atrial flutter. PGS001263 has the tightest phenotype alignment to the target (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both “Atrial flutter”), while PGS001339/PGS001841/PGS002050 use the broader combined endpoint “atrial fibrillation and flutter” (including a time-to-event variant for PGS001339). However, the best-endpoint-aligned model (PGS001263) comes from a known pan-trait sparse UKB framework (“across 813 traits”, snpnet; ~2k variants), which domain knowledge flags as tending to generalize poorly; the two portability-framework alternatives (PGS001841/PGS002050) are also not disease-focused and lack PRS-comparable metrics like PGS-only AUROC/R² in the provided metadata, limiting comparability. Given this mix of endpoint advantage but framework/sparsity concerns and missing comparable performance for the non-snpnet models, a direct match exists but the evidence is not strong enough for a clearly high-quality pick."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are concept-aligned to autism spectrum disorder via trait_reported/trait_efo, but endpoint fidelity favors PGS000327 because its phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Autism spectrum disorder,” whereas PGS002790’s phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn),” indicating a less direct/clean endpoint despite the ASD label. PGS000327 also comes from an ASD-focused GWAS meta-analysis publication context (“Identification of common genetic risk variants for autism spectrum disorder.”) with basic covariates (PCs, genotyping wave) and reports an OR (1.33) and variance explained metric (R²=0.0245) in European validation, providing more directly comparable disease-focused evidence than PGS002790, which reports only partial R² (0.00546) and no effect size, with many validation records tied to a broader cross-disorder/sex-specific study. Main limitation: neither model reports PRS-only AUROC/R², and both are EUR-only evaluation, so generalizability and PRS-isolated performance remain uncertain."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002034",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001826, PGS002034), both aligned to the same EFO target (congenital vitamin K-dependent coagulation factors deficiency) with identical reported/phenotyped endpoint wording and the same study context (UKB-based portability paper; same covariates; same training cohort; same ancestry distribution; same validation size). With endpoint fidelity effectively tied, selection turns on method/structure and comparable PRS metrics: PGS002034 uses LDpred2 with many more variants (32,552 vs 45) and has essentially identical partial correlation performance (0.0563 vs 0.0562 in European validation). However, both candidates come from a portability/framework-style publication and only report weak PRS-comparable metrics (partial-r; no AUC/R2), limiting the strength of the recommendation; thus the best available direct match is PGS002034 but overall quality remains sub-optimal given the sparse performance evidence."
}
